     chain has no FOURTH rule whose own gate is checked against a value written two-or-more layers
     further out than its immediately adjacent neighbor - every rule's own OutputHeadFeatures value
     is checked only by the rule immediately enclosing it, whose own RequiredHeadFeatures asks for
     exactly that value. So the union Add ever builds is consulted by nothing downstream, and
     PriorityUnion's narrower single value and Add's wider set both still satisfy the one gate that
     does look at them. No stale branch is exposed; this is a straight-line derivation with no
     "reset via an empty-required rule to a different value that something further in still needs"
     shape at all.

     mrP (innermost): no RequiredHeadFeatures, OutputHeadFeatures grade=two. Suffix "a".
     mrQ: RequiredHeadFeatures grade=two, OutputHeadFeatures grade=three. Suffix "b".
     mrR (outermost): RequiredHeadFeatures grade=three, no OutputHeadFeatures. Suffix "c".

     Forward: nep -mrP(out=two)-> nepa -mrQ(req=two matches;out=three)-> nepab -mrR(req=three
     matches)-> nepabc.

     Analysis (unapply order R,Q,P): R contributes three (from empty, both modes agree). Q's gate
     checks out=three vs fs=three (trivial, both). Q's fold (req=two) diverges the SAME way g2's
     mrC does (Add keeps {three,two}, PriorityUnion narrows to two) - but nothing further in ever
     asks about "three" again: P's own gate checks out=two, which both the Add set and the
     PriorityUnion singleton satisfy, and P's own req is empty, so the derivation reaches the root
